<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>On-device LLM inference survey</title>
<style>{{ css | safe }}</style>
</head>
<body>
<header>
  <h1>On-device LLM inference survey</h1>
  <p class="pitch">Load time, time-to-first-token, generation speed, and memory
    for small LLMs on consumer machines, measured with the same pinned
    llama.cpp build everywhere.
    <a href="{{ project_url }}">Methodology &amp; source</a>.</p>
  {% if not empty %}
  <div class="chips">
    {% for s in stats %}<span class="chip"><b>{{ s.v }}</b> {{ s.k }}</span>{% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% endif %}
</header>

<details class="cta">
  <summary><b>Add your machine</b>
    <span class="fine">one command · 15–60 min · ~8&nbsp;GB download</span></summary>
  <div class="cta-body">
    <div class="cmd"><span class="os">Linux / macOS</span>
      <code id="cmd-bash">{{ install_bash }}</code>
      <button class="copy" data-copy="cmd-bash">copy</button></div>
    <div class="cmd"><span class="os">Windows</span>
      <code id="cmd-ps">{{ install_ps }}</code>
      <button class="copy" data-copy="cmd-ps">copy</button></div>
    <p class="fine">One script: downloads a prebuilt kit and the models
      (~8&nbsp;GB), measures for 15–60 minutes, and produces a ~300&nbsp;KB
      results file to attach to a
      <a href="{{ project_url }}/issues/new?template=submission.yml">submission issue</a>.</p>
  </div>
</details>

{% if empty %}
<section><p class="fine">No submissions yet.</p></section>
{% else %}

<section>
  <h2>Results</h2>
  <p class="fine">One model at a time. Each row is one lane — one device on one
    machine — and color says its device class, the same three hues everywhere on
    the page. The bands are device classes; inside a band, lanes are sorted
    by generation speed. Both tok/s rates fall as the context fills, so a lane
    spans the range the sweep measured across depth — deep end slow, empty end
    fast — and the numbers at the end read the two ends out. Hover a row for
    those ends with the depths they were measured at, and for which machine the
    lane runs on. The two tok/s columns are on log scales — the lanes span three
    orders of magnitude, and a linear axis reads as one discrete GPU and a wall
    of slivers. The dotted lines on them are reading-speed references, each
    named under the axis; a reference is drawn only where it fits the current
    scale.</p>
  {% if controls %}
  <div class="controls" id="controls">
    {% for c in controls %}
    <label>{{ c.label }}
      <select data-signal="{{ c.signal }}">
        {% for o in c.options %}
        <option value="{{ o }}"{% if o == c.value %} selected{% endif %}>{{ o }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
      </select>
    </label>
    {% endfor %}
    <span class="fine">scopes this page</span>
  </div>
  {% endif %}
  <div class="island" data-spec="grid"></div>
</section>

{% if tasks %}
<section>
  <h2>What a task costs</h2>
  <p class="fine">The same lanes, in seconds for the task below. Nothing here is a
    new measurement: each bar reads the sweep's own measured cost curves for that
    configuration, and past the depth a lane was measured at the last measured rate
    is carried on and the bar is marked as an estimate — the only wall is the
    context the model itself was trained for. Pick a task or type your own; the
    model and device selectors above scope this chart too.</p>
  <details class="fine">
    <summary>How to read these bars</summary>
    <p>The prompt is priced off the sweep's measured time-to-first-token curve —
      what the sweep itself clocked to reach that depth, interpolated between its
      points — and the reply is integrated over the measured generation rate as the
      context fills, so a long reply is never priced at its opening rate. A half-lit
      bar rests on something thin: a lane whose sweep reached a single fill, or a
      task deeper than the sweep went — there each curve's last measured rate is
      carried on past its evidence, which may still be optimistic. A row with no bar
      has nothing to evaluate: either the lane measured no curve, or the task does
      not fit the model's trained context. The dotted lines on the generation column
      are the reading-speed references from above. Hover any row for its value —
      estimates wear a <code>~</code> — and what an estimate rests on.</p>
  </details>
  <div class="controls" id="task-controls">
    <label>task
      <select id="t-preset">
        {% for t in task_list %}
        <option value="{{ t.key }}"{% if loop.first %} selected{% endif %}>{{ t.label }}</option>
        {% endfor %}
      </select>
    </label>
    <label data-field="depth">context before
      <input type="number" id="t-depth" min="0" max="131072" step="64"
             value="{{ task_list[0].depth }}">
      <span class="words" data-for="t-depth"></span></label>
    <label>prompt
      <input type="number" id="t-prompt" min="0" max="131072" step="64"
             value="{{ task_list[0].prompt }}">
      <span class="words" data-for="t-prompt"></span></label>
    <label>reply
      <input type="number" id="t-out" min="0" max="8192" step="64"
             value="{{ task_list[0].out }}">
      <span class="words" data-for="t-out"></span></label>
    <span class="fine">counts in tokens · about ¾ of a word each</span>
  </div>
  <p class="fine" id="task-note"></p>
  <div class="island" data-spec="tasks"></div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if accuracy %}
<section>
  <h2>How accurate are those predictions?</h2>
  <p class="fine">Every submission runs one fixed validation job — a 1,550-token
    prompt and a 256-token reply from an empty context — and the calculator above
    prices that same shape from the sweep's measured curves alone. Each dot is one
    lane: how far the prediction lands from what the job measured there. Time to
    first token checks the prompt pricing, the whole task adds generation and
    reads the bottom line; hover a dot for its lane and the two numbers behind
    it. What is left in these bars is mostly how differently
    the two runs were measured — the sweep is one long instrumented pass, the job
    a short burst — since inside the measured range the prediction is the sweep's
    own clock. Generation is not graded on its own, because on lanes whose sweep
    reached a single fill the job's own rate is part of the decode ladder, and the
    comparison would not be independent.</p>
  <div class="island" data-spec="accuracy"></div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if launch %}
<section>
  <h2>First launch</h2>
  <p class="fine">What the same selection pays once, before any of the rates above
    apply. Pipeline compilation is the GPU driver building this model's compute
    pipelines from source, from an empty shader cache; the driver keeps the result,
    so it is a once-per-machine cost rather than something every launch pays. It is
    an estimate, and the subtraction behind it is worth knowing: the survey tool builds
    those pipelines by running tokens through every dispatch width it will later
    use, so the span it times is compilation and inference together — usually
    mostly inference. What that width walk costs as plain prefill is priced from
    the lane's own cost function and taken back off. Hover a bar for the raw span
    and the amount netted out. Two numbers of similar size make a small difference,
    so read a small bar as small rather than as exact. Cold first touch is the load
    with the page cache cold — the weights read off storage plus the context
    allocation that follows. The survey tool measures it once per machine and model, on
    the lane that loaded the file first, so it appears on that lane and is not
    spread across the ones that later read the same warm file. CPU lanes compile
    nothing for inference and are absent, so a CPU-only selection leaves this chart
    empty. Some platforms pin no shader cache at all — macOS keeps Metal's under OS
    control, and the Windows vendor drivers honour none of the variables the
    survey tool sets — so there the driver may have handed back pipelines the machine
    built earlier. Those are marked with an asterisk: a floor of unknown tightness,
    and not comparable across machines.</p>
  <div class="island" data-spec="launch"></div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if curves %}
<section>
  <h2>Cost curves</h2>
  <p class="fine">The same selection, over depth: prompt-reading time vs prompt
    length (log ms), and generation speed vs context already used (log tok/s).
    These are
    the points the intervals above are drawn from. Color is the device class;
    hover a point for its lane. A hollow mark is a lane the sweep's budget
    reached at one depth only: a measurement, not a slope.</p>
  <div class="curves">
    {% for sid in curves %}<div class="island" data-spec="{{ sid }}"></div>{% endfor %}
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if memory %}
<section>
  <h2>Memory</h2>
  <p class="fine">Two things, for the same selection. Left: what the allocator
    reserved at each context size it was measured at. These are the allocator's own
    totals, so they are exact — there is no spread to show. The dashed line is the
    weights floor, which does not move with context; the gap up to the solid line is
    what the context costs, KV cache plus compute buffers. Two lanes that reserved
    the same amount draw one line over the other — the reservation follows the model
    and the context size more closely than the device. Right: what the process
    actually held while the job ran. The bar is peak resident memory, the dot above
    it is the device-local pool on the lanes where the platform reports one per
    process — a DRM-backed GPU holds that next to its host memory, so both marks are
    real — and the thin dashed marker is the allocator total from the left chart, at
    the deepest context it measured, as a reference. On unified-memory lanes there is
    one pool and the bar already contains all of it; a lane whose per-process device
    memory was not measured says so rather than showing zero.</p>
  <div class="curves">
    {% for sid in memory %}<div class="island" data-spec="{{ sid }}"></div>{% endfor %}
  </div>
</section>
{% endif %}

{% if threads %}
<section>
  <h2>Thread width</h2>
  <p class="fine">CPU lanes only — a GPU lane's thread pool serves leftover host-side
    work and says nothing about the device. Each phase was measured at the width the
    lane runs and at halves of it, and each is drawn in its own unit: milliseconds for
    one fixed prompt chunk from an empty cache, tok/s for one fixed generation burst at
    an already-filled context. Those work units are small and are not the ones the
    charts above use, so read ratios between widths within a chart — never a number
    here against the generation rates in Results. The dots are the widths that ran and
    the line between them is interpolation, not a fit; the hollow ring is the width
    the lane actually runs, which is the width every other number on this page comes
    from. All the lanes here are CPUs and share one hue, so point at a curve: it
    keeps its ink while the others recede, its dots name the lane on hover, and its
    steps label themselves with what each one bought against a perfect division of
    the work — one lane at a time, because all of them at once is more annotation
    than chart. The two phases behave differently on purpose: prompt
    reading keeps dividing its work, so it keeps rewarding cores, while generation
    shares one memory path and pays less for each core added.</p>
  <p class="fine">Nothing here is extrapolated. The ladder walks down from the width
    the OS picked for the lane, so it stops at the widest width that lane will run —
    which is exactly where a fitted ceiling would have no evidence under it. What a
    wider width would buy is not measured, so it is not claimed, and how much of a
    machine the ladder covered depends on the platform: linux and windows hand out
    every physical core, macOS only the top performance cluster.</p>
  <div class="curves">
    {% for sid in threads %}<div class="island" data-spec="{{ sid }}"></div>{% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% if thread_scalars %}
  <div class="scroll-x">
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>lane</th><th>width run</th>
      <th>widths measured</th><th>notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
    {% for t in thread_scalars %}
      <tr><td>{{ t.lane }}</td><td>{{ t.width }}</td>
        <td>{{ t.measured }}</td><td>{{ t.note }}</td></tr>
    {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  {% endif %}
</section>
{% endif %}

<details class="fold">
  <summary>Reference data<span class="hint">{{ tabs | map(attribute='hint') | join(' · ') }}</span></summary>
  <div class="tabs" role="tablist">
    {% for t in tabs %}
    <button type="button" role="tab" class="tab{% if loop.first %} on{% endif %}"
            aria-selected="{{ 'true' if loop.first else 'false' }}"
            aria-controls="p-{{ t.id }}" data-panel="p-{{ t.id }}">{{ t.label }}</button>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  {% for t in tabs %}
  <div class="panel" id="p-{{ t.id }}" role="tabpanel"{% if not loop.first %} hidden{% endif %}>
    <p class="fine">{{ t.about }}</p>
    {% if t.id == 'machines' %}
    <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>machine</th><th>CPU</th><th>GPU</th><th>RAM</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      {% for m in machines %}
        <tr><td>{{ m.machine }}</td><td>{{ m.cpu }}</td><td>{{ m.gpu }}</td><td>{{ m.ram }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    {% elif t.id == 'ceilings' %}
    <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>lane</th><th>GEMM TFLOP/s</th><th>d2d GB/s</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      {% for c in ceilings %}
        <tr><td>{{ c.lane }}</td><td>{{ c.gemm }}</td><td>{{ c.d2d }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    {% elif t.id == 'unusable' %}
    <div class="scroll-x">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>lane</th><th>machine</th><th>model</th><th>quant</th><th>status</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
      {% for u in unusable %}
        <tr><td>{{ u.lane }}</td><td>{{ u.machine }}</td><td>{{ u.model }}</td><td>{{ u.quant }}</td><td>{{ u.status }}</td></tr>
      {% endfor %}
      </tbody>
    </table>
    </div>
    {% elif t.id == 'samples' %}
    {% for c in completions %}
    <p class="fine">{{ c.who }}</p>
    <pre>{{ c.text }}</pre>
    {% endfor %}
    {% endif %}
  </div>
  {% endfor %}
</details>
{% endif %}

<footer>
  <p class="fine">Built {{ built }} ·
    <a href="{{ project_url }}">{{ project_url }}</a> ·
    every submission includes its raw measurement traces.</p>
</footer>

{% for sid, spec in specs.items() %}
<script type="application/json" data-island="{{ sid }}">{{ spec | tojson }}</script>
{% endfor %}
<script type="application/json" id="palette-dark-map">{{ palette_dark_map | tojson }}</script>
{% if tasks %}
<script type="application/json" id="task-pack">{{ task_pack | tojson }}</script>
<script>{{ tasks_js | safe }}</script>
{% endif %}
<script>{{ vega_js | safe }}</script>
<script>{{ report_js | safe }}</script>
</body>
</html>
